{"response_id":"717fd674-4a8a-48bc-8587-3a02616a0554","sections":[{"view_model":{"__typename":"GenAISingleLayoutViewModel","primitive":{"__typename":"GenAIBotThinkingStatusPrimitive","title":"Show thinking","is_in_progress":false,"icon":"THINKING","target_secondary_screen_id":"DETAILS","target_secondary_screen_tab_id":"STEPS","show_answer_now":false}}},{"view_model":{"__typename":"GenAISingleLayoutViewModel","primitive":{"__typename":"GenAIMarkdownTextUXPrimitive","text":"Yes, but unevenly - and yes, WWII does overshadow colonialism in both collective memory and school syllabi in Europe. This is a well studied gap.

### 1. Do Europeans learn about colonialism as perpetrators?

It depends heavily on the country and on the school:

**UK:** The National Curriculum is very flexible. The only compulsory history topic is the Holocaust. It is entirely possible to not teach colonisation, the Transatlantic Slave Trade or the British Empire at all. There is a non-statutory option on migration but little required on how Britain was influenced by the wider world. In recent years there have been proposals to increase black history and empire history, but it is still up to the individual school. {{IE_0}}5f55{{/IE_0}}

**France:** Comparatively more coverage. The French curriculum teaches different European empires - Portuguese, Belgian, British, Dutch - in a comparative manner. But the narrative for long focused on "civilizing mission" and decolonization wars like Algeria are still politically sensitive. {{IE_1}}f7ee{{/IE_1}}

**Belgium, Netherlands, Portugal, Spain:** Historically very little. Belgium's atrocities in Congo were barely mentioned until the 2000s. Portugal only started serious reckoning after 1974. Austria's curriculum only from 2016 onwards explicitly requires teachers to address the consequences of colonialism and imperialism and to reflect critically on racist notions. {{IE_2}}5bc9{{/IE_2}}

**Germany:** Colonialism - like the 1904-08 Herero and Nama genocide in Namibia, recognized as genocide by Germany only in 2021 - was almost absent because post-war German memory culture was entirely built around responsibility for Nazism.

So the general pattern: universities and left-leaning media discuss it a lot, but average public knowledge comes more from museums, debates about statues, and social media than from compulsory schooling. Polls in the UK, France and Netherlands consistently show large minorities think empire was something to be proud of, which historians link to lack of critical teaching.

### 2. Does WWII overshadow colonialism?

Yes, for three reasons that researchers point to:

**a) WWII is the founding myth of modern Europe.** The memory of World War II is described as omnipresent across Europe. The European Union's own legitimacy is built on "never again" - overcoming Nazism, fascism, and later communism. {{IE_3}}3cb1{{/IE_3}}

Scholars argue the European project has a fixation on a rather narrow reading of WWII. The European Parliament's resolutions on remembrance explicitly talk about crimes of Nazi, fascist and communist totalitarian regimes as the core of shared memory. {{IE_4}}a09b{{/IE_4}}

**b) Competition between memories.** EU reports note there continues to be a latent competition and partial incompatibility between different memory frames and remembrance cultures in Europe. Colonial memory does not fit neatly into the WWII anti-fascist frame, because colonialism was perpetrated by the Allies themselves - Britain, France, Belgium, Netherlands - who are the "good guys" in WWII. {{IE_5}}faae{{/IE_5}}

**c) Chronology and proximity.** WWII is within living family memory, has films, memorial days, Holocaust education in every country, and is legally enforced remembrance. Colonialism ended 60-80 years ago and affected peoples far away. It's easier to externalize.

This is changing slowly since 2020:

- UK, France, Belgium have introduced new museum narratives - e.g. Royal Museum for Central Africa in Belgium reopened with a critical lens
- European Parliament called for revising history curricula to place greater focus on slavery, colonialism and fascism {{IE_6}}b930{{/IE_6}}
- Germany, Netherlands now include colonial history in some state curricula

But in most European classrooms today, a student will spend weeks on WWI trenches, Weimar, Nazi rise, Holocaust and Cold War, and maybe 1-2 lessons on empire - if at all.","inline_entities":[{"key":"IE_0","metadata":{"__typename":"GenAISearchCitationItem","reference_id":1,"reference_url":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/03054985.2022.2087618","reference_title":"Increasing inclusion for ethnic minority students by teaching the British Empire and global history in the English history curriculum","reference_display_name":"tandfonline.com","sources":[{"source_type":"THIRD_PARTY","source_display_name":"tandfonline.com","source_subtitle":"Increasing inclusion for ethnic minority students by teaching the British Empire and global history in the English history curriculum","source_url":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/03054985.2022.2087618","favicon":{"url":"https://external.xx.fbcdn.net/emg1/v/t13/6440039883035947520?stp=dst-src&url=https%3a%2f%2fwww.google.com%2fs2%2ffavicons%3fdomain%3dwww.tandfonline.com%26sz%3d128&utld=google.com&ccb=13-1&oh=06_Q3_DAdlYd8pWSihkQPybaZ-qXm83i9uR8tKLPnew61aQBMsZ&oe=6AA9B02A&_nc_sid=a4bca5","width":80,"height":80}}],"reference_favicon":{"url":"https://external.xx.fbcdn.net/emg1/v/t13/6440039883035947520?stp=dst-src&url=https%3a%2f%2fwww.google.com%2fs2%2ffavicons%3fdomain%3dwww.tandfonline.com%26sz%3d128&utld=google.com&ccb=13-1&oh=06_Q3_DAdlYd8pWSihkQPybaZ-qXm83i9uR8tKLPnew61aQBMsZ&oe=6AA9B02A&_nc_sid=a4bca5","width":80,"height":80}}},{"key":"IE_1","metadata":{"__typename":"GenAISearchCitationItem","reference_id":2,"reference_url":"https://indianexpress.com/article/research/why-history-of-british-empire-is-not-taught-enough-in-uk-schools-8327905/lite/","reference_title":"Why history of British Empire is not taught enough in UK schools","reference_display_name":"indianexpress.com","sources":[{"source_type":"THIRD_PARTY","source_display_name":"indianexpress.com","source_subtitle":"Why history of British Empire is n