management delays"],"markdown_cells":[{"text":"Christmas Island Pipistrelle"},{"text":"Last bat seen Aug 26, 2009"},{"text":"Unknown multi-factor - invasive yellow crazy ants, wolf snakes, habitat loss from phosphate mining"},{"text":"Invasive species introduced by humans, mining operations, Australian management delays"}]}]}}},{"view_model":{"__typename":"GenAISingleLayoutViewModel","primitive":{"__typename":"GenAIMarkdownTextUXPrimitive","text":"Causes and timeline are documented in contemporary surveys and IUCN assessments:

The baiji was concluded to be likely extinct due to unsustainable by-catch and was described as the first cetacean species to be driven to extinction by human activity, with deaths caused by boat strikes and fishing gear accidents as well as degradation due to pollution and droughts. {{IE_0}}0290d955{{/IE_0}}

Western black rhino saw a 96% decline between 1970 and 1992 with widespread poaching concluded to be partly responsible and was officially declared extinct in 2011. {{IE_1}}369033d1{{/IE_1}}

The Bramble Cay melomys data point to human-induced climate change being the root cause of the loss, and it was described as almost certainly caused by ocean inundation and probably represents the first recorded mammalian extinction due to anthropogenic climate change. {{IE_2}}0beb83de{{/IE_2}}

Chinese paddlefish extinction was concluded to be mainly due to overfishing and dam construction, with the main cause the construction of Gezhouba Dam in 1981 fragmenting the population and blocking migration and annual harvests reaching 25 tons by the 1970s. {{IE_3}}10c61110{{/IE_3}}

Lonesome George's population was wiped out after the introduction of goats, with overexploitation by whalers and fishermen, habitat destruction, and introduction of invasive species like goats causing extinction. {{IE_4}}dc3634b6{{/IE_4}}

The golden toad extinction coincided with an exceptionally dry interval caused by the 1986–1987 El Niño and has been linked to the interaction of global warming and the introduced chytrid fungus Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis. {{IE_5}}e4ab43d0{{/IE_5}}

Spix's macaw was thought to be extinct in the wild because of pet trade trapping and loss of dry Caatinga forests and became extinct in the wild primarily due to poaching and habitat loss. {{IE_6}}36c6068b{{/IE_6}}

Northern white rhinos populations were destroyed by rampant poaching for horns valued in traditional Asian medicine and were considered extinct in the wild by 2008. {{IE_7}}b87b5301{{/IE_7}}

The thylacine was hunted to extinction due to belief it killed sheep and a government bounty was awarded with the bounty scheme initiated by the Tasmanian government in 1888. {{IE_8}}66fae3a12b8f{{/IE_8}}

Christmas Island pipistrelle was declared officially extinct with only one individual remaining in 2009. {{IE_9}}0781d4b3{{/IE_9}}

### Pattern of responsibility

**1. State / infrastructure projects:** Baiji and Chinese paddlefish - Chinese state dam building on the Yangtze. No single corporation was prosecuted; it was state-planned industrialization.

**2. Organized criminal networks + consumer countries:** Western black rhino and northern white rhino - not one company, but transnational gangs feeding demand. Demand hubs were Vietnam, China for traditional medicine, and Yemen for ornamental dagger handles.

**3. Diffuse global - climate:** Bramble Cay melomys and golden toad are not traceable to one company. IPCC lists climate change as a key driver for the golden toad, making fossil-fuel economies collectively responsible.

**4. Small groups of individuals + local industry:** Spix's macaw - a handful of trappers and wealthy private collectors in Brazil, Germany and elsewhere in the 1980s-90s removed the last wild birds. Pinta tortoise - fishermen and whalers introduced goats for food, which then exploded to 40,000+.

**5. Government policy failure:** Thylacine - the Tasmanian government paid £1 per head. That bounty, more than disease, is credited with the final collapse.

